<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Acute nephritic syndrome in a 28-year-old composer and pianist: swelling of the face and legs, cola-colored urine, blood pressure 162/100, and creatinine 1.62 mg/dL, two to three weeks after a febrile sore throat, with aching knees and ankles. Please confirm the cause, advise whether a kidney biopsy is needed, and guide fluid and blood pressure management and follow-up. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two days. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is this post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, or does anything point to another glomerular disease?</item><item>Does he need a kidney biopsy now, and what would make one necessary later?</item><item>Can he be managed as an outpatient, and how should fluid and blood pressure be treated?</item><item>Do his joint pains after the streptococcal infection mean acute rheumatic fever, and does he need an echocardiogram or penicillin prophylaxis?</item><item>How should he be monitored, and for how long, and what is the outlook for his kidneys?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Mozart, a 28-year-old composer and pianist, is seen at the request of Dr. Kranzler-Obi two days after he presented with swelling and dark urine. Two to three weeks ago he had a febrile sore throat that was not treated. About ten days later his knees and ankles ached, without swelling, and he had three days of crampy abdominal pain with vomiting and a low fever. Four days before the first visit his eyelids and ankles began to swell and his urine turned the color of cola. Since starting furosemide he has passed more urine and lost 1.2 kg; the urine is lighter but still brownish. The abdominal pain and vomiting have stopped and the joint aches are easing. He has a mild morning headache without vision change. He has had no rash, hemoptysis, sinus disease, or previous episode of dark urine. He took ibuprofen for five days before the first visit and has stopped it. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart was born in Salzburg on January 27, 1756, the son of Leopold Mozart, a musician in the service of the Archbishop of Salzburg. Of the seven children of his parents&apos; marriage only two survived: his sister Maria Anna, born July 30, 1751, and Wolfgang. [D]</item><item>In Vienna in late 1762, on the family&apos;s second journey, an attack of scarlet fever kept him in bed for a fortnight. [D]</item><item>At The Hague in the autumn of 1765, after his sister&apos;s dangerous illness, he was seized with a violent attack of fever that reduced him to extreme weakness for several weeks; he kept writing on a board laid across his bed. [D]</item><item>In 1767 the family fled a smallpox outbreak in Vienna to Olmütz, but he and then his sister caught smallpox there; the family was taken in by Count Leopold Anton von Podstatzky, Dean of Olmütz, and his illness was so severe that he lay blind for nine days and had to protect his eyes for several weeks afterwards. [D]</item><item>In the spring of 1783 he fell ill with an illness his biographer calls cholera, during an epidemic in Vienna. [D]</item><item>In a letter to his daughter dated September 14, 1784, Leopold Mozart wrote that his son had been very ill in Vienna: he had become overheated at Paisiello&apos;s new opera and had gone out into the open air to find the servant who had his overcoat, and this &quot;brought on rheumatic fever, which without careful attention might have turned to typhus.&quot; He quoted his son&apos;s letter: &quot;I have had raging colic every day for a fortnight at the same hour, accompanied by violent vomiting,&quot; and that his doctor, Sigmund Barisani, visited him almost daily. [D]</item><item>In 1784, up to the time of the episode, he lived at Graben 29 in Vienna. [D]</item><item>His mother died in Paris on July 3, 1778, while traveling with him, after an illness of some weeks during which she was bled; the cause is not recorded in the cited source. [D]</item><item>One author argues that he had frequent attacks of tonsillitis throughout his life and that in 1784 he developed a post-streptococcal Schönlein-Henoch syndrome. [R]</item></list><paragraph>10 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

